# What are this endpoint's verdicts? One row per metric measured in the most
# recent run that ran anything for this endpoint: the metric id, the dqv:value
# verdict, the conformance level where the metric carries one (only
# sw:metric:service-description does today), and how long the probe took.
# Also returned, in the SAME result set but distinguishably: any metric that
# run recorded as sw:NotMeasured rather than measured, with the reason it was
# declined: "cost-ceiling" for a metric priced out of the run, or
# "prober-failed" for an endpoint the prober never got to ask at all.
#
# ?endpoint is the input. It is substituted with a NamedNode before execution
# (SEP-0007 substitution, which is why it appears in the SELECT projection:
# pyoxigraph only substitutes projected variables), so it is never
# interpolated into this text. See web/queries/endpoint_content.rq's header
# for the full reasoning; this query reuses it rather than repeating it.
#
# A MEASURED row has ?verdict bound (and, where the graph has them, ?level
# and ?elapsedMs); ?reason is unbound. A DECLINED row has ?reason bound;
# ?verdict, ?level and ?elapsedMs are unbound. A caller must read those as two
# different shapes, never as one: "we chose not to run this metric" and "we
# ran it and got this verdict" are different facts, and the graph is explicit
# about which applies to a given metric on a given run. Collapsing a declined
# row into an ordinary measurement (say, by treating ?reason as if it were a
# verdict) would state a confident value for a metric nobody measured that
# run.
#
# "Most recent run" is decided by the run's activity's prov:generatedAtTime, a
# typed xsd:dateTime, exactly as in endpoint_content.rq, and for the same
# reason: today's run IRIs happen to embed an ISO-8601 instant, so string
# ordering would agree by coincidence, and would silently break the day that
# shape changes.
#
# It is expressed as "no newer run recorded anything (a measurement or a
# decline) for this endpoint" rather than as an ORDER BY ... LIMIT 1 subquery,
# and for the identical reason recorded in endpoint_content.rq's header: the
# value substituted for ?endpoint does not reach inside a subquery's own
# projection, so the subquery would pick the newest run in the whole store and
# then fail to join whenever that run did not happen to touch the endpoint
# being asked about. At 548 endpoints, an endpoint whose probe timed out or
# whose sweep failed in the latest run hits exactly that case, routinely
# rather than rarely. FILTER NOT EXISTS is evaluated against the current
# solution, so ?endpoint stays bound inside it and the comparison stays
# per-endpoint.
#
# The run chosen is the one that recorded ANYTHING for this endpoint, measured
# or declined, not the newest run per individual metric: one sweep either
# measures or declines every metric it applies to a given endpoint in the same
# pass, so an endpoint's measured and declined rows from one run always share
# that run's single prov:Activity. Mixing "most recent per metric" in here
# would let a caller see metric:availability from tonight's run beside
# metric:classes from last week's, two different sweeps' opinions presented as
# one, which is exactly the blended answer endpoint_content.rq's header warns
# against for content samples.
#
# Two more things are returned, per row and constant within a run, and they
# are not about any metric: whether the run these facts come from finished,
# and what the newest run in the store says. Stage 1c-b4 writes each
# endpoint's facts as that endpoint finishes, so a store can hold a run that
# stopped halfway, and a reader shown that run's facts is entitled to know it
# stopped. The three facts that stage writes are sw:emission "incremental" on
# the activity (the header, so a reader knows to expect chunks and a footer),
# sw:completedEndpoint per endpoint the run finished, and sw:finalised true as
# the last line a finished run ever writes. A run carrying NONE of them is a
# run from before that stage: it promised nothing, so its missing sw:finalised
# says nothing either, and this query returns the absence rather than a
# verdict about it.
#
# THE NEWEST-RUN BRANCH IS NOT PER-ENDPOINT, and it is the only thing in this
# query that is not. Everything else here has been scoped to one endpoint
# since stage 2-1, including the selection of the run whose facts are
# returned, which is unchanged: ?run is still the newest run that recorded a
# measurement or a decline FOR THIS ENDPOINT, chosen by the FILTER NOT EXISTS
# below, and nothing in the newest-run branch touches it.
#
# The widening is deliberate, and it buys the case the per-endpoint selection
# cannot see. A run that dies partway records nothing at all for every
# endpoint it never reached, so those endpoints are invisible to a
# per-endpoint question: their newest recorded facts are an older, complete
# run's, and a page built from those facts alone shows them with no hint that
# tonight's sweep died before getting here. At 548 endpoints that is most of
# them, and it is a regression on what this page said before stage 1c-b4, when
# a failed endpoint carried prober-failed declines in the newest run and the
# rows read "not measured". So the newest activity in the store is selected on
# its own, with its sw:emission, its sw:finalised where it has one, and its
# sw:completedEndpoint for THIS endpoint where it has one. Those are the
# inputs; a caller derives "a newer run died before reaching this endpoint"
# from them.
#
# The branch is OPTIONAL so that an endpoint's verdicts still come back from a
# store where no activity can be found. What it costs is measured rather than
# reasoned about: see the note on its subquery below for the numbers and for
# why the shape of that subquery is the load-bearing part.

PREFIX dqv: <http://www.w3.org/ns/dqv#>
PREFIX prov: <http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#>
PREFIX sw: <urn:sparqlwatch:>

SELECT ?endpoint ?run ?generatedAt ?metric ?verdict ?level ?elapsedMs ?reason
       ?emission ?finalised
       ?newestRun ?newestGeneratedAt ?newestEmission ?newestFinalised
       ?newestCompleted
WHERE {
  GRAPH ?run {
    ?activity a prov:Activity ;
              prov:generatedAtTime ?generatedAt .
    {
      ?measurement a dqv:QualityMeasurement ;
                   dqv:computedOn ?endpoint ;
                   dqv:isMeasurementOf ?metric ;
                   dqv:value ?verdict ;
                   prov:wasGeneratedBy ?activity .
      OPTIONAL { ?measurement sw:level ?level }
      OPTIONAL { ?measurement sw:elapsedMs ?elapsedMs }
    }
    UNION
    {
      ?notMeasured a sw:NotMeasured ;
                   sw:notMeasuredOn ?endpoint ;
                   sw:notMeasuredMetric ?metric ;
                   sw:notMeasuredReason ?reason ;
                   prov:wasGeneratedBy ?activity .
    }
    # This run's own account of how it was written and whether it finished.
    # Both OPTIONAL, because a run from before stage 1c-b4 carries neither and
    # its verdicts are still the answer to the question that was asked.
    OPTIONAL { ?activity sw:emission ?emission }
    OPTIONAL { ?activity sw:finalised ?finalised }
  }
  FILTER NOT EXISTS {
    GRAPH ?newerRun {
      {
        ?newerThing dqv:computedOn ?endpoint
      }
      UNION
      {
        ?newerThing sw:notMeasuredOn ?endpoint
      }
      ?newerActivity a prov:Activity ;
                     prov:generatedAtTime ?newerGeneratedAt .
    }
    FILTER (?newerGeneratedAt > ?generatedAt)
  }

  # The newest run in the store. See this query's header: this is the one
  # selection here that is not scoped to ?endpoint, and the per-endpoint
  # selection above is untouched by it.
  OPTIONAL {
    # "Newest" as an aggregate subquery, which is the one place in this file
    # where a subquery is the right shape rather than the wrong one. The
    # objection recorded in endpoint_content.rq's header is that the value
    # substituted for ?endpoint does not reach inside a subquery's projection,
    # so a subquery that has to be per-endpoint silently stops being one. This
    # one is per-STORE by design: it mentions no endpoint, projects one
    # variable, and the ?endpoint-scoped lookup stays outside it.
    #
    # Measured on this machine, one endpoint's eight rows, over a store built
    # from the committed fixtures plus N activity-only run graphs:
    #
    #                               402 runs    1002 runs
    #   without this branch           163 ms       976 ms
    #   with it, this form            217 ms      1288 ms
    #   with it, FILTER NOT EXISTS   1898 ms      not run
    #
    # The aggregate on its own is 0.4 ms at 402 runs and 1.0 ms at 1002, so
    # finding the maximum is not what either form costs. What the FILTER NOT
    # EXISTS form costs is comparing every run against every other run once
    # per row, and 1.9 s is a page that times out on a store holding a year of
    # nightly sweeps. So the shape here is load-bearing rather than a matter of
    # taste. The growth in the first row belongs to the per-endpoint selection
    # above and predates this branch.
    {
      SELECT (MAX(?anyGeneratedAt) AS ?newestGeneratedAt) WHERE {
        GRAPH ?anyRun {
          ?anyActivity a prov:Activity ;
                       prov:generatedAtTime ?anyGeneratedAt .
        }
      }
    }
    GRAPH ?newestRun {
      ?newestActivity a prov:Activity ;
                      prov:generatedAtTime ?newestGeneratedAt .
      OPTIONAL { ?newestActivity sw:emission ?newestEmission }
      OPTIONAL { ?newestActivity sw:finalised ?newestFinalised }
      # Scoped to ?endpoint even inside the branch that is not, because what
      # is wanted is whether THIS endpoint was finished. ?endpoint is
      # substituted before execution, so this is an index lookup rather than a
      # scan of the run's several hundred completion markers.
      # ?newestCompleted exists only so the match is visible in the result: an
      # OPTIONAL that binds no new variable leaves a caller unable to tell
      # whether it matched.
      OPTIONAL {
        ?newestActivity sw:completedEndpoint ?endpoint .
        BIND (?endpoint AS ?newestCompleted)
      }
    }
  }
}
